More Vulnerabilities That Can Be Paid (281–290)
281. Unprotected Admin Panel
Admin Panel Exposure
Administrative dashboards are accessible without authentication or strong protection.
💰 Typical payout: $500 – $10,000
282. Debug Information Disclosure
Debug Information Disclosure
Applications reveal stack traces, server paths, or internal code details.
💰 Typical payout: $100 – $2,000
283. Cross-Origin Resource Sharing Misconfiguration
CORS Misconfiguration
Improper CORS policies allow unauthorized domains to access sensitive data.
💰 Typical payout: $300 – $5,000
284. Insecure Deserialization
Insecure Deserialization
Applications deserialize untrusted data, potentially leading to remote code execution.
💰 Typical payout: $1,000 – $15,000
285. HTTP Request Smuggling
HTTP Request Smuggling
Attackers manipulate HTTP request parsing differences between servers.
💰 Typical payout: $1,000 – $20,000
286. Host Header Injection
Host Header Injection
Improper validation of the Host header allows attackers to manipulate application logic.
💰 Typical payout: $300 – $5,000
287. Improper Security Headers
Missing Security Headers
Important headers such as CSP or X-Frame-Options are missing or misconfigured.
💰 Typical payout: $50 – $1,000
288. Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking
WebSocket Hijacking
WebSocket connections lack proper authentication or origin checks.
💰 Typical payout: $300 – $5,000
289. Sensitive Data in Client Logs
Sensitive Data Logging
Applications log sensitive information like passwords or tokens.
💰 Typical payout: $200 – $3,000
290. Improper API Error Handling
API Error Information Disclosure
Error responses reveal sensitive information about backend systems.
💰 Typical payout: $100 – $2,000
